<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kronstadt rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kronstadt rebellion was a revolt in March 1921 by sailors, soldiers and workers of the naval fortress of Kronstadt near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> against the Bolshevik government. Once celebrated supporters of the revolution, the rebels demanded freely elected soviets, freedom of speech and an end to grain requisitioning. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> crushed the revolt by attacking across the ice, but it helped persuade Lenin to adopt the New Economic Policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kronstadt-rebellion.jpg" alt="Kronstadt rebellion" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kronstadt_attack.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Kronstadt, on Kotlin Island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>, was the main base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-fleet">Baltic Fleet</a> and defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>. Its sailors had taken part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> and the civil war, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a> had earlier called them the adornment and pride of the revolution. The island held about 50,000 civilians and 26,000 sailors and soldiers, most of them veterans of 1917.</p><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> ended, the country was in ruin, and war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and the forced seizure of peasants' grain deepened the hardship. A January cut of one third in bread rations led to demonstrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and strikes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>, where about 60 percent of the large factories closed for lack of fuel. When the crews of the Petropavlovsk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a> heard of the repression in Petrograd, they passed a resolution of fifteen demands, including new elections to the soviets by secret ballot, freedom of speech and press for workers, peasants, anarchists and left socialists, and the release of political prisoners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The rebels called their movement a third revolution, meant to restore power to freely elected soviets. They sought reform of the Bolshevik party, not its end, and rejected help from émigrés. The authorities presented them as agents of the Entente and counter-revolution, and issued an ultimatum on 5 March. Operations began on 7 March, when some 60,000 troops took part. Tukhachevsky's units advanced over the ice in a snowstorm; many soldiers drowned, others defected or refused to advance, and the attack failed.</p><p>On 16 March Tukhachevsky's reinforced army of 50,000 prepared to take the island and its 15,000 rebels, beginning with heavy artillery fire. Cadets and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> units attacked before dawn, and after heavy fighting on 17 March the last northern forts fell by 1 a.m. The revolt was suppressed on 18 March after it had lasted sixteen days. No power agreed to give the rebels military support, and no aid reached the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>There were no public trials. Of about 2,000 prisoners, 13 were tried in private as leaders and sentenced to execution two days after the fall of Kronstadt. Hundreds of rebels were killed at Kronstadt and hundreds more shot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>, and the rest were sent to prisons and forced labour camps, where many died of hunger or disease.</p><p>Lenin called the crisis the most critical the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> had yet faced. The rebellion did not meet its demands, but it hastened the New Economic Policy, which replaced war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-goldman">Emma Goldman</a> and other supporters of the regime lost their faith in it. Historian Paul Avrich judged that the rebels had little chance of success.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kronstadt rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Kronstadt</b> sailors’ revolt of March 1921 was crushed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Once the Bolshevik government was secure it harshly suppressed them, including during the <b>Kronstadt rebellion</b>, and crushed the anarchist movement in Ukraine as well, while many workers turned to the Communist parties.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Syndicalists, who denounced the regime as state capitalist, were harassed and arrested, and the arrests that followed the <b>Kronstadt rebellion</b> of 1921 ended the movement.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/syndicalism">Syndicalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The suppression of the <b>Kronstadt rebellion</b>, which Trotsky as leader of the Red Army and the other Bolsheviks repressed brutally, remained a point of attack.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/trotskyism">Trotskyism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
